    <h1 class="headline">India's cyber-crime map just crossed a line</h1>

    <div class="utility-row">
      <span class="source-line">Source: <b>NCRB "Crime in India 2024"</b> via MHA (PIB)</span>
      <button class="listen-btn" data-article="brd-cyber"><span class="bars"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class="lbl-text">Listen to this article</span></button>
    </div>

    <figure class="article-image">
      <img src="/assets/images/cybercrime.jpg" alt="A hooded figure using a laptop against projected computer code" width="1200" height="628" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
      <figcaption>Registered cybercrime passed one lakh cases in 2024.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="visual-block">
      <div class="visual-label">Cyber-Crime Breakdown</div>
      <div class="visual-frame">
        <div class="kpi-row">
          <div class="kpi k-purple"><div class="kn">1,01,928</div><div class="kl">cases in 2024 — first time past one lakh</div></div>
          <div class="kpi k-red"><div class="kn">+104%</div><div class="kl">rise since 2020 (~50,000)</div></div>
          <div class="kpi k-blue"><div class="kn">~48%</div><div class="kl">are fraud or cheating</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="toggle-row" id="cyber-toggle">
          <button class="toggle-btn" data-set="states" data-active="true">Top states</button>
          <button class="toggle-btn" data-set="growth" data-active="false">Fastest-growing</button>
          <button class="toggle-btn" data-set="heads" data-active="false">By crime type</button>
        </div>
        <div id="cyber-bars"></div>
        <div class="impact-readout" id="cyber-impact"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="visual-caption">Registered cases only. Switch views to see where the crime is, where it is growing fastest, and what it actually consists of.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="supporting-copy">
      <p>Registered cyber-crime in India crossed <span class="stat st-purple">one lakh cases</span> for the first time in 2024 — 1,01,928 of them, roughly double the 50,000-odd recorded in 2020. Two states carry almost half the national total between them: Telangana at 27,230 and Karnataka at 21,993.</p>
      <p>But the useful finding is not where the crime is. It is what the crime <i>is</i>. Computer-related offences, fraud and cheating account for most of it. Obscene content, stalking and everything else make up a small remainder. This is a money story, not hacking in the Hollywood sense — which makes it a consumer-protection and banking-supervision problem at least as much as a policing one.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <span class="co-k">Read the numbers carefully</span>
      <p>These are <b>registered</b> cases. Differences between states partly reflect how diligently each state books a complaint, not just how much crime happens there. Between 2020 and 2024, Assam fell by roughly 88% and West Bengal by roughly 60% — a fall that says far more about registration practice than about anyone becoming safer.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="supporting-copy">
      <p><b>If it happens to you:</b> financial fraud can be reported on helpline <b>1930</b>, or at cybercrime.gov.in. The I4C says more than <span class="stat st-blue">₹11,158 crore</span> has been saved across 32.8 lakh complaints, with coordination hubs now covering hotspots including Mewat, Jamtara, Hyderabad and Guwahati. Speed matters more than anything else here. The money can be recovered only before it is moved on.</p>
    </div>
